  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nToday through next Friday...Troughing will increases across the \nregion this weekend.  This will force a frontal boundary south into \nand through the forecast area today into tonight.  This, along with \nthe addition of daytime heating, will aid in the development of \nshowers and storms across the CWA into this evening.  A storm or two \ncould become intense enough during this time, and produce gusty \nwinds.  Frequent lightning and heavy downpours can be expected with \nall of today's convection.\n\nWhile hot humid conditions will continue today, cloud cover \nassociated with said convection, and slightly drier air filtering \ninto the region in the front's wake, will provide some temporary \nrelief from the oppressive heat that's gripped the region for \nsometime.  Still, a \"Limited Threat\" for dangerous heat stress will \nexist across much of the the CWA this afternoon.  Here, the \ncombination of highs in the low to middle 90s and dew points in the \nlow to middle 70s, will yield afternoon heat index values near 105F. \nAgain, the slightly drier air that advects into the area in the wake \nof the front will hang around the region Sunday into Monday.  Highs \nwill generally range from the upper 80s to mid 90s each day, with \ndew points from the upper 60s to lower 70s.\n\nUnfortunately, this'll all be short-lived as we move into the mid \nand latter portions of the week.  Strong high pressure aloft will \nagain re-assert itself across the Lower Mississippi River Valley \nduring this time.  High temperatures will again begin climbing back \ninto the mid and upper 90s areawide.  Moisture levels will also \nbegin creeping up, possibly again leading to another bout of \ndangerous heat stress as we end the forecast period.  Rain chances \nalso will be tough to come by during this time, with only an \nisolated shower or storm possible. /19/",
